WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
Welcome to Nostalgia.

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.040
In this episode, we're talking about baseball.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:27.020
Alright, you're here on Nostalgia, and this is the chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:33.540
chronological meaning of course, in order, and nothing would sound better than another NES launch game, right boys?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.820
That's right, another sports game at that.

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:36.940
Another sports game at that.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.940
Now just for the record, just to clear up a little something here in terms of chronologicalness,

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:42.620
if that's a word that you can say.

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:47.200
Nope. Okay, cool, you can't say that, but we did because you can't tell us what to say on the radio.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.740
In terms of chronologicalness, you know, you might be wondering like, oh, was baseball really the second game?

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:59.120
Like, no, they all came out on the same day, and that day is actually like, kind of up for grabs,

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:02.220
because retailers didn't really like, release things at the same,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:05.200
there was no agreed upon date for things back then.

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:12.900
So the general census is October 1985, on a specific day, you could go to your Toys R Us or what, you know.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.520
Circuit City. Circuit City, that's a great one.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:16.820
I have a Circuit City story, maybe for the post show.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.240
And you could just kind of, you know, pick it up, the NES.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.180
And that was with these launch games.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.100
And so we're kind of, when they're falling on the same day, or they don't have a specified day,

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.760
they just have a specified month, we're just going to do the one that goes in alphabetical order.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.000
So first was 10-yard fight, which is a number.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.760
Numbers always come before letters.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.840
We're learning. That's just a given law in society.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.080
They don't teach it at the school. That's one of those things you've got to figure out.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.480
That's a screen skill.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:44.780
I learned that the hard way.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.660
You accidentally do it, you go to jail, and then when you come out, you're like, okay, now I know.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:49.800
Mm-hmm. Cool.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:57.580
So, very quickly, let's just go around the room for those of us who don't, well, we know each other,

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.760
but for the people at home who don't know us, let's talk about who we are.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:01.920
I'm Michael Esposito.

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:03.380
I'm Joe Castu.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:04.620
I'm Sam Smith.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.000
And I'm Sean Tresmond.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.320
Alright, guys, let's get right into it and talk about baseball.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.760
That was the name of the game.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.140
There was no fun.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:22.960
I love how definitive it is.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:24.240
Like, this is baseball.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:27.240
It's like, all of a sudden, when you get the card, you're like, oh my god, baseball.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.780
It's also very hard to Google.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.180
They just, yeah. It's also just as boring as regular baseball.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:35.800
Boom! Sam, do you want to take us through initial impressions?

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:37.820
Yeah, I mean, it's not that it's boring.

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.720
So, what I do before all of these is I look up the manual, right?

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:46.900
And I was staring at that thing for like 10 minutes, I think, just trying to make sense

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:52.700
of it. Not that it was difficult, but there's just so many different things that you need to learn

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:57.400
how to do that I feel like, I mean, like, maybe if you've played baseball games before,

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.080
you would know, but just kind of hopping into this.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:03.080
Some things just kind of, you had to...

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.580
So, you didn't know how to do it. You don't really come from a baseball background.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.200
Like, you didn't know the rules necessarily?

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.380
Or you feel like you still knew the rules, but there was just a lot of controls to learn?

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.700
Oh, the controls I'm talking about.

00:03:11.740 --> 00:03:12.740
I know how baseball works.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.060
Alright, cool. Nice. So, yeah, I actually really liked kind of the controls.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.280
I did it a little differently because I went in without looking at the manual, which is

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.800
what I did with 10-yard fight, which worked fine for me.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.100
But with this, I was like, at first I kind of was thinking like, oh, so there's a lot

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.980
you don't have control of when you're fielding.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:33.740
Until I looked at the manual and I was like, oh, they actually kind of came up with solutions

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:37.280
for all the like, all the limitations that the controller had.

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.880
Like, a lot of solutions for like, I mean, you could try and pick off the runner, which

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.900
I didn't think you'd be able to do because there's only two buttons on the controller.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.220
Which is something that the computer tries to do an awful lot.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:48.900
The computer does try and do that a lot.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:50.300
Sorry, I'm getting ahead of my...

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.080
My initial impression was I was actually, I think I liked it more than 10-yard fight.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.500
I was pretty impressed. Okay, Sean?

00:03:55.500 --> 00:04:01.420
All right, creative solutions to control issues and trying to simulate as much of baseball

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:07.560
as possible. Aside, I rage quit this game through my first actual game.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.100
Through your initial impression, a rage quit happened.

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.380
Yeah. All right, I love that.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:13.520
I can't wait to talk to you about the rest of this game.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.100
Cool. And then just real fast, my initial impression is, yeah, this is baseball.

00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:20.040
Like, definitely baseball.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.960
And to Sam's point, this is like, for people who think baseball isn't fun, this enforces

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:27.280
that rule. Yeah, it says like...

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:29.060
This does not sell you a lot of baseball.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:34.580
It doesn't. Right, I can agree that if you already like baseball, I don't know, maybe, I mean, you

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:35.740
like baseball. I think baseball is fun.

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.060
You maybe didn't like this game as much, but I kind of liked the game.

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.520
I thought it was, yeah, like you said, it was baseball.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:46.120
They accentuate all the boring parts of baseball.

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:47.660
So let's get into it then.

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.240
Let's just jump right in there.

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:53.120
And Joe, you kind of seem to be the person who liked this game the most.

00:04:53.260 --> 00:04:54.140
So defend it.

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:58.500
Yeah. So I don't want to say it was like absolutely amazing or anything, but I was just as a launch

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.320
NES title.

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.780
I mean, I was, I thought it was pretty cool that you could, you know, everything that

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.580
you could do, which again, was kind of reinforced by the fact that I didn't look at the manual

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.540
at first. So then when I discovered it all, I was like, oh my God, you can actually throw different

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:13.580
pitches or you can, you know, even just like there are issues with fielding definitely,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:16.760
especially with like how slow the fielders are.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.420
And the fact that you don't control the fielders.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:23.720
Yeah. It blew my mind when I really, I thought I must be doing something wrong.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:24.700
That was part of it.

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:29.680
I was not reading the manual, but I was picking it up as I was going along as far as I could

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:32.380
play without actually quitting the game.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:33.520
Did you ever read the manual?

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:38.440
Never read it. There is a lot of like multi button things that like I never would have guessed.

00:05:38.440 --> 00:05:39.880
I'd love to hear what you have to say.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:48.240
Whenever it went into the field, I wasn't screaming at my TV, but it's definitely the most clumsy

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:50.780
fielders I've ever seen in a video game.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:56.540
I know that they're trying to show you that your left and like your midfielder, your left

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:57.900
fielder, your right fielder are far away.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.740
So they're not moving very fast, but they still have to show up on the screen.

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:02.740
But like, they're not moving.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:04.060
They're not even moving towards the ball.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:05.900
Yeah. That's the most frustrating part.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.840
I agree with that. It's like all of a sudden it feels like, oh, this is a, and this is actually something

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.260
different than I guess than what I'm about to talk about.

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:13.320
But I was thinking like, oh wow, this is a pop up.

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:16.440
Like, no worries. Right. And it's like, but my player's not getting that.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:18.580
Yeah. You know, it was like, what's going on?

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.680
Like you hit the ball and it seems like, you know, it's going to be the same thing.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:23.960
Like, oh, they're never going to get that thing.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:26.520
And then they just like scoop it up and they throw it to first and you're out.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:33.160
I wonder, I don't know the difference in difficulty and like if it can get, if it gets any different.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.020
But for me, the AI in this game was challenging.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:42.120
See, I had a kind of a weird experience because my first game, I was really good at bat.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:45.760
Like it was, I hit a home run, like every other pitch was a home run.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:47.920
And then I wasn't so great in the field.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:50.160
So it ended up being like a really high scoring game.

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:52.760
And then my second game, I mean, I had picked a different team.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:54.880
I don't know if they got as in depth to make, I doubt it.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.520
I think it was just something, something that with the way I was playing.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.860
But the second time I was way better in the field and I was terrible at bat.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.660
I kept grounding out.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.580
I'm pretty much every, every pitch I grounded out.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:06.980
Did you, did you move around the batter's box at all?

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:08.740
I did once and I never did.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:09.840
I tried it.

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:12.460
I don't know if it was like helping me with my reach or anything.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.800
Yeah. I don't know what it was to help me.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:19.780
If you're on an inside pitch and you don't have enough time to move your guy like further outside the box.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.820
And I, and it was hard enough trying to just read like, is this going to be a ball or is this going to be a strike?

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.480
And forget about like your own pitching.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:28.300
Awesome. I have no idea if I can throw a ball.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:29.880
Yeah. There's also like, I think I can only throw strikes.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:33.040
When your guy swings the bat, you have to make, you have to hold down the button.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.340
Otherwise it doesn't swing all the way, but you can't check swing.

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.380
So like what, there's like,

00:07:36.380 --> 00:07:38.760
I think that has to do more with where you're trying to hit the ball.

00:07:39.000 --> 00:07:41.140
Like, I, I, yeah, that's what, that was my guess.

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:49.980
Honestly, I think that the moving around the batter's box is like an archaic version of like the guest pitch feature in a lot of the recent games.

00:07:49.980 --> 00:08:01.040
I do think, yeah. And I think like also there might, there might be like some kind of like, if you move further out of the batter's box so that your thicker part of your bat hits the ball, it might go out further.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:02.880
That's what I'm thinking for like inside pitches.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:04.960
Right. Like outside pitches seem fine.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:08.120
I don't know. There's so much this game doesn't communicate to you.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:13.260
Another big thing for me was sometimes my pitcher would, you know, like look like he's ready.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:16.580
You know, he's looking at the catcher for the read and he shook his head like, no.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.660
And I'm like thinking like, is he telling me that he doesn't want to throw this?

00:08:19.660 --> 00:08:22.660
I knew about the D pad that you can change the pitches.

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.700
Yeah, you can change the pitch you want. And so I was like, does he not want the fastball?

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:27.900
Like, is he saying that the AI is going to hit this better?

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:29.640
I think it's just like, never communicated.

00:08:29.920 --> 00:08:32.060
I think what happens is, well, I thought that too.

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:44.920
I think what happens is, the pitcher just like has a motion that he does, where he just like, the animation is he bends forward and he just kind of shakes his head a couple times and then he goes up into his position and then you can pick the pitch.

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:46.060
I think he's looking at runners.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.960
No, and then he picks off the runner and then he picks off the runner again and then he throws the pitch.

00:08:49.960 --> 00:08:51.200
No, no, your pitcher I'm saying.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:53.880
Oh, okay. When you're pitching, you can't just pitch.

00:08:54.180 --> 00:09:00.620
You have to wait like six seconds for your guy to finish shaking his head and then get on the mound and then you can pitch.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.860
And it's like, why? I already know what I want to do.

00:09:02.980 --> 00:09:06.420
It's just, it's like those boring moments they kept in baseball.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.900
Yeah. Like all the waiting.

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:11.240
Simulation. Yeah. All the waiting around for things to happen.

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:13.280
That's in this. That's the part that you like.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:21.100
I didn't mind it. What I did mind is that I couldn't tell if it was just a cool little neat feature of like, hey, this is just like the real game where the pitcher looks at the catcher.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:22.960
Right. Here we go again.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.560
I don't think baseball is that boring.

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:26.700
If you're there, if you're watching it, then like maybe.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:32.500
Okay. There's a lot going on in baseball, which is why I liked that there was a lot going on in this.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.180
But I mean, I'm defending it a lot, but it definitely had a lot of issues.

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.500
There were a lot of like really interesting things that they were able to put into this game.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.500
But it just, I think it just highlights the parts that they weren't able to get into it.

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:52.140
Like the fielding is just so, like I can pick what kind of pitch I want and where I can control the pitch after I let go of the ball.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:56.460
But I can't field, you know, it's like I can't move anyone in the outfield.

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:00.920
I had no idea how to advance runners.

00:10:01.300 --> 00:10:06.320
This is something I should have like read the manual for, but every hit was a single.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:14.180
And it had something to do, and I'm trying to remember exactly, but you, on the D-pad you press the base you want to go to and hold that and press B.

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.340
And then they'll go to, and that's how you throw to those bases too.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:18.900
It sends a runner at that base.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:21.400
This probably explains the rage quit a little bit.

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:23.800
Do you want to tell us a little bit about this game that you have?

00:10:23.800 --> 00:10:28.240
Yeah, I started off normal enough for the first inning was scoreless.

00:10:28.380 --> 00:10:30.620
And then the second inning I was down 1-0.

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:33.680
And then the third inning I'm down 5-0.

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.340
Which is fine, I had a bad inning.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:37.940
And then I just couldn't hit.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:39.640
I got a couple singles.

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:43.480
And then in the fourth inning I'm down 15-0.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:46.440
And that's where I stopped playing the game.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:53.040
So I played it enough to absorb all of these cool like, oh yeah, you could do this and that and the other thing.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:55.600
But I played it a lot less than you guys.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.140
I think despite the frustrations I still had a pretty fun first game.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:02.320
I think the games are pretty intense.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:05.900
But yeah, no. In a sense, I'm sorry, intense isn't the right word there.

00:11:06.080 --> 00:11:09.060
What I mean is, you're never like comfortable in this game.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:11.260
Like you might think, oh I'm up by seven runs.

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.620
I got this in the bank. But then your fielder will just like chase after the ball.

00:11:14.620 --> 00:11:22.780
Chase after the ball. But so my game, I was going and they scored a little bit because I was trying to figure out the controls and everything.

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:25.980
Even though I looked at the manual. And then I kind of got the hang of it.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.860
I scored. I got a home run that felt really good.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:29.220
I got a second home run at one point.

00:11:29.320 --> 00:11:38.400
That was pretty sick. It was going back and forth a little bit for a while as I was kind of getting used to the controls.

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:43.620
And then going into the final inning, into the ninth inning, I tie the game to score.

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:48.920
And then there, I'd score at the tying run and then I'm out.

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:51.480
So the score is like six to six or something like that.

00:11:51.940 --> 00:11:56.700
I have to hold their team before I can go into the tenth inning and do extra innings.

00:11:57.100 --> 00:12:00.120
Which I assumed there would be, but there are no extra innings if you tie.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:01.320
You just tie.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:02.760
I was so frustrated.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:05.420
I was like okay, and I finally did it.

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:08.660
I was like oh, I actually held off the opponents.

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:12.500
When you tie, it actually goes to a black screen that says you're on your way to the Super Bowl.

00:12:12.500 --> 00:12:14.080
Yeah. It's so bad.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.140
When you tie, it's like well, that's the game.

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.120
I'm like that's not the game.

00:12:17.520 --> 00:12:19.140
Were these released together actually?

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:20.380
Sorry, this and 10 yard fight?

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:21.780
Were they like on one cartridge?

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.140
Same. Oh no, no, no, they were not.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:25.420
No, no, no, yeah, yeah, yeah. They were separate cartridges.

00:12:25.600 --> 00:12:28.260
You were paying full money for each of these.

00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:37.520
And I think talking about baseball, the high scoring games for a second, my game final score was like 14 to 13.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:40.800
And I think that that's way too many runs in it.

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.680
And that's the normal occurrence in these games too.

00:12:43.900 --> 00:12:46.980
It's like the fielders are just so bad in both teams.

00:12:47.280 --> 00:12:55.180
The fielders are just so bad that it just makes you like, I don't know, it just, it's like any time I think I'm doing good, it's like nah, I'm just gonna blow up.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:13:02.060
It is a trade off. It, it, it, you, they have all of these little things that remind you of how slow baseball is.

00:13:02.280 --> 00:13:12.900
They have, you have to wait for the, the pitcher to, to crash down, get stretched out, look at it, look at all the runners, talk, like have a mini conversation with his catcher.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:17.240
